  "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2000 \\\\ 10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >2000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >200 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(200) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(200) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(200)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(200)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(200)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\u224814.067.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $15$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2000.$",
